    "scenario": true,
    "text": "An architect needs to implement a rollback strategy for database deployments. A production deployment accidentally dropped a critical table 20 minutes ago. The table had a DATA_RETENTION_TIME_IN_DAYS of 1. How can the architect recover the table?",
    "options": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Restore from Fail-safe by contacting Snowflake support"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Use UNDROP TABLE to recover the table from Time Travel"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Re-create the table from the most recent COPY INTO backup"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Use zero-copy cloning of the production database from before the drop"
      }
    ],
    "correctAnswers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanation": "A table dropped within the Time Travel retention period can be instantly recovered using UNDROP TABLE. Since the table was dropped only 20 minutes ago and retention is 1 day, UNDROP TABLE will fully restore it. Fail-safe (A) is only used after Time Travel expires and requires Snowflake support. Restoring from a COPY backup (C) is unnecessary when UNDROP is available. Cloning from a point before the drop (D) would work but is more complex than UNDROP.",
    "source": "UNDROP TABLE",
    "sourceUrl": "https://docs.snowflake.com/en/sql-reference/sql/undrop-table",
